@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

#
# prima-materia — two searches carried in the Swedenborgian corpus
#
# Both came back empty and both empty results are load-bearing, which is unusual
# enough to say at the top. The first asked for the sign by which a living man
# would know the state the doctrine makes decisive, and the corpus states in one
# place that there is none and nowhere offers one. The second asked for any
# operation at all, and there is none anywhere in eight volumes. A holding that
# only recorded what this corpus says would carry a large body of reports; what
# these two searches add is that it teaches nothing to do and promises nothing to
# notice.
#
# The two together are also what places this transmission on the awareness axes
# at pmc:ApertioIsNotByEffort: with no operation in the corpus there is nothing
# on the acquired side of that axis for it to stand at, and the placement rests
# on the absence as much as on the grammar of the passages.
#

pmp:SwedenborgSignumAperitionisSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the corpus names a sign by which a living man knows his interiors are opened"@en, "Ob das Korpus ein Zeichen nennt, an dem ein Lebender die Öffnung seines Inneren erkennt"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Swedenborg, De caelo et inferno, in the English of the Swedenborg Society, Heaven and Hell, London 1885, and in the Society's edition of 1893; Swedenborg, Arcana coelestia, volumes I, V, XI and XII of the Standard Edition in the English of John Faulkner Potts; Swedenborg, Sapientia angelica de divino amore et de divina sapientia, in the English of the Swedenborg Society, London 1912; Swedenborg, Diarium spirituale, volume I, in the English of Bush and Smithson, London 1883 — eight volumes, each searched as one text" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Any mark, sign, perception or test by which a man still living could establish that the spiritual or celestial degree has been opened in him, or that his interiors have been opened so that he may speak with spirits and angels. The doctrine makes that opening the condition of everything the corpus claims, and the question is whether it also gives the man a way of knowing where he stands. If it does not, then the central claim of the largest body of first-person reports in this holding is one its own author says nobody can check on himself." ;
    pm:searchTerms "All eight volumes normalised to bare letters first, then searched for: may know that his interiors (no hits in any volume); knows that his interiors (none); sign of the opening (none); know whether the spiritual degree (none); perceive the opening (none); is sensible of the opening (none); no knowledge of the opening (one hit, in Angelic Wisdom, and it is the negative statement itself). Then the passage that hit was read continuously together with the thirty numbers around it, 232 to 262, and the four Arcana places on the internal respiration were read for the same question." ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-29" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-29" ;
    skos:note "Nothing, and the one hit is the corpus saying so. So long as a man lives in the world he has no knowledge of the opening of these degrees within him, because he is in the natural degree and the spiritual does not communicate with it by continuity but by correspondences, and communication by correspondences is not sensibly felt. The same withdrawal is made of the internal respiration, which is called internal and for that reason imperceptible to man. The node is pmc:SwedenborgNullumSignumInVita."@en ;
    skos:note "Why this is an exhibited gap and not bookkeeping. A reader who works forward through these volumes meets a great many reported experiences and will not notice that none of them is offered as a criterion; the absence is only visible if somebody asks for the criterion and comes back empty. Anybody who owns the Latin, or the eleven volumes of the Arcana that were not opened, can now try to close it, and the question is stated precisely enough to be answered."@en ;
    skos:note "The limit of the search is that it was carried over English translations and over four of the twelve Arcana volumes. Against that stands what the corpus does state in the volumes read: not a silence but an explicit denial, which a translator would have had to render somehow and which a further volume would have to contradict rather than merely supplement."@en .

pmp:SwedenborgPraxisSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the corpus anywhere gives an operation by which the converse could be brought about"@en, "Ob das Korpus irgendwo eine Operation angibt, durch die der Verkehr herbeizuführen wäre"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Swedenborg, De caelo et inferno, in the English of the Swedenborg Society, Heaven and Hell, London 1885, and in the Society's edition of 1893; Swedenborg, Arcana coelestia, volumes I, V, XI and XII of the Standard Edition in the English of John Faulkner Potts; Swedenborg, Sapientia angelica de divino amore et de divina sapientia, in the English of the Swedenborg Society, London 1912; Swedenborg, Diarium spirituale, volume I, in the English of Bush and Smithson, London 1883 — eight volumes, each searched as one text" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Any instruction by which a second person could set about the thing the corpus reports: a posture, a regulation of the breath, an abstinence, a fast, a vigil, a recitation, a formula, a period of preparation, a discipline of any kind, or a sentence of the form let him who desires this do that. The question decides whether this transmission carries a pm:Practicing node at all, and therefore whether it belongs among the transmissions this holding can compare operation for operation with the others." ;
    pm:searchTerms "All eight volumes normalised to bare letters, then searched for: let him who desires (no hits anywhere); if any one wishes to speak with spirits (none); in order to speak with spirits he must (none); by means of abstinence (none); regulate his breathing (none); method by which (two hits, both in Arcana volume XI and both inside the exposition of the curtains of the tabernacle, neither an instruction); hold the breath (one, in Angelic Wisdom, and it is an argument that a man who holds his breath altogether can think only in his spirit, not an exercise); fasting (one, in Arcana volume XII, and not of this); discipline (three, one of them a translator's preface about the march of the truths of heaven); posture (seven, every one of them either a printing of imposture or a description of how a body lay or was tormented); exercise and practise (about eighty between them, all read at the hit and every one of them either a faculty being exercised, a device being practised upon somebody, or a virtue being practised)." ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-29" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-29" ;
    skos:note "Nothing. In eight volumes there is no operation, and that is why traditions/swedenborgian.ttl carries no pm:Practicing node. The one passage that comes nearest is a prohibition and it runs the other way: those who think much on religious subjects and do not break the current of their thoughts with various useful occupations become visionaries and enthusiasts who take every spirit they hear for the Holy Spirit. The corpus describes the way into the experience in order to forbid it."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the finding that makes the pair with the Theravada handbook read in the same batch worth having. That one is operation throughout and states its conditions of failure as properties of the circumstances; this one has capacities, prerequisites, cautions and a criterion, and nothing to do. Two transmissions at opposite ends of the same axis, both now in the holding at the wording, and the axis itself — whether a transmission supplies a procedure — is not yet anything the graph can be asked about."@en ;
    skos:note "It also settles what the internal respiration is in this corpus, against the whole later reception of it. There is no breathing exercise here: the respiration is reported as led from within without any exertion of the will, as imperceptible, and at one place as arriving unasked at the moment of an attack and ending it. The nodes are pmc:SwedenborgRespiratioInterna and pmc:SwedenborgRespiratioProtegens."@en .
